{# ---- the reverse list. One card, one item per line, no elaboration.

   TEST BEFORE ADDING A LINE: is this something Mastodon actually does,
   or just something of ours that isn't finished? Both belong here, but
   they are not the same claim and the copy must not blur them. Cut on
   sight anything that is neither: "no per-user federation allow-list"
   was here once, and Mastodon has no such thing either, so it was pure
   padding. A reader who spots one invented difference stops believing
   the rest of the page.

   `byChoice` marks a deliberate trade-off rather than unfinished work. ---- #}
{% set gaps = [
  { text: "<b>Bonfire's Circles don't federate yet; Mastodon's Collections do.</b> 4.6 shipped curated, shareable sets of profiles that travel across servers as ActivityPub <code class=\"font-smono text-[.85em]\">featuredCollections</code>, with consent built in: you opt in to being featured, you're told when you're added, and you can take yourself out. A Bonfire circle stays local, though it can already contain people from any instance, and boundary interaction policies do hold off-instance. Their FEP is the shape ours should grow into." },
  { text: "<b>Parts of the Mastodon-compatible API are still missing.</b> Scheduled posts, filters, announcements, suggestions, quote-approval requests and the admin surface have no endpoints yet, so some clients will degrade." },
  { text: "<b>No official native mobile app.</b> Bonfire installs to your home screen as a progressive web app, and third-party fediverse clients are an option, but there's no Bonfire-native iOS or Android app." },
  { text: "<b>There's more to set up.</b> Bonfire is a kit you assemble rather than one fixed product, and that flexibility costs configuration effort Mastodon doesn't ask of you.", byChoice: true },
  { text: "<b>A decade less hardening at scale.</b> While Bonfire 1.0 has been load-tested in production, Mastodon has had years of use by instances small and large." }
] %}

